00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:08.240
Aim training doesn't transfer because the entire&nbsp;
approach it reinforces is incorrect because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:15.360
aim trainers. Most people aim like this. They&nbsp;
will see someone, they will stop, then they aim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:23.680
then they shoot. They stop, aim, shoot. Aim&nbsp;
trainers condition you to only be comfortable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:31.920
with aiming while you're standing still. However,&nbsp;
in this gap between stopping and shooting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:40.320
you are choosing to be a sitting duck. And if&nbsp;
you aren't fast enough at this aim portion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.720
you're essentially just letting them kill you.&nbsp;
And most people would think, "Oh, I just need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.920
to be faster. You know, let's aim train so I&nbsp;
can minimize the downtime or let's play hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:58.160
bots so that I can learn to be faster. Let's aim&nbsp;
faster." But that thought process is inherently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:06.080
flawed because there will always be downtime if&nbsp;
you aim after you stop. Think about this question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:12.800
What is the point of stopping if I'm not ready&nbsp;
to shoot? The correct approach is to move the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:23.920
aiming portion to before you stop. So instead of&nbsp;
stop, aim, shoot, it becomes aim, stop, shoot.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:30.160
When you don't choose to become a stationary&nbsp;
target, you become way harder to kill. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.520
from the enemy perspective, you kill them the&nbsp;
instant that you stop moving. And to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:41.040
it feels like they had no chance. As opposed to&nbsp;
this perspective where I actually feel like I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:47.600
have a chance to shoot back at them. So, how&nbsp;
do you change this? How do you go from stop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:58.560
aim, shoot to aim, stop, shoot? So what I do&nbsp;
personally kill 50 or 100 bots in the range. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:06.080
then one rule is always be strafing. Then just aim&nbsp;
at the bot. And when you think you can kill him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:15.360
stop and shoot. Stop and shoot. Stop and&nbsp;
shoot. Good cue here is that when I'm accurate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:21.200
my gun will pop out. See how when I'm&nbsp;
moving, the gun is towards me. When I stop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:31.360
it pops out. That's a good indicator of&nbsp;
when you can actually shoot accurately.

